(4537439, 647896, 41116, '<td valign="top">Wow! Not bad for your first challenge entered. Keep it up!</td>', 'Wow! Not bad for your first challenge entered. Keep it up!', '2008-03-04 11:43:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(4537489, 647896, 51538, '<td valign="top">Well done on your first entry.  I remember letting lose a little over a year back.  I learned a lot from that first one and have learned quite a bit here at DPC since then.  Have fun and keep shooting.  In NYC we have a lot of people covering downtown and the Brooklyn Bridge - not to mention all of those out of towners who interlope in from time to time and take shots of our territory - but few people covering the GW and uptown.  Looking forward to seeing more of your work...</td>', 'Well done on your first entry.  I remember letting lose a little over a year back.  I learned a lot from that first one and have learned quite a bit here at DPC since then.  Have fun and keep shooting.  In NYC we have a lot of people covering downtown and the Brooklyn Bridge - not to mention all of those out of towners who interlope in from time to time and take shots of our territory - but few people covering the GW and uptown.  Looking forward to seeing more of your work...', '2008-03-04 11:59:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(4537804, 647966, 3746, '<td valign="top">One of the best images on DPC ever in my opinion.. truely inspiring.</td>', 'One of the best images on DPC ever in my opinion.. truely inspiring.', '2008-03-04 14:47:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(4537809, 647896, 91017, '<td valign="top">New York is one of my favorite places to go. I love it. You done an amazing job capturing this bridge. This picture is beautiful, the blue in the water is wonderful.</td>', 'New York is one of my favorite places to go. I love it. You done an amazing job capturing this bridge. This picture is beautiful, the blue in the water is wonderful.', '2008-03-04 14:49:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(4537957, 648971, 30049, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/><u>First Impression</u> <br/> <br/>Tension.  Apprehension.  Who left the door open?  Who''s in the house now?<br/> <br/>What does this have to do with leading lines?<br/> <br/>So many questions???<br/> <br/><u>Looking Deeper</u> <br/> <br/>This image triggered several responses when I first opened it, as mentioned above.  From the context of leading lines I wonder how many questioned where the lines lead?  Just a thought.<br/> <br/>Looking at this a little longer I can see where the lines are pointing to the opening at the bottom.  ???  Wait.  That''s not a threshold.  ???  Ok.  Just read the your notes in the Photographer''s Comment section (I usually wait to read those and comments until after I''ve gathered some thoughts).  That''s the top of the door.  Clever.  :-)  Still feels like someone''s in the house...<br/> <br/>Getting past the trickery and looking at the photo itself.  I like the angles formed by the door and how the bottom right third is separated from the rest.  Personally, I find my attention going to the door handle first before I move anywhere else.<br/> <br/>In reading the comments I see some questioning the color (i.e. WB off, lose the yellow, etc...).  I''m not quite so bothered by the color, but it does make me wonder (strongly) how this would feel in B&amp;W.  Although B&amp;W may add to the sense of doom and mystery.<br/> <br/>The other technicals (lighting, focus, DOF) all work fine from where I''m sitting.<br/> <br/>Certainly an interesting take on the challenge.  Could be good for a revisit if the POV challenge comes back around, eh?  :-)<br/> <br/>Oops!  One other thing I just noticed as I scrolled up to take a last look at the photo dimensions and file size...did you consider cropping about 1/5 of the image from the top?  I think it would have remained balanced, but when you resize then the image would look larger.  Something to think about.<br/> <br/>Questions?  Feel free to send me a PM.  Thanks!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club First Impression  Tension.  Apprehension.  Who left the door open?  Who''s in the house now? What does this have to do with leading lines? So many questions??? Looking Deeper  This image triggered several responses when I first opened it, as mentioned above.  From the context of leading lines I wonder how many questioned where the lines lead?  Just a thought. Looking at this a little longer I can see where the lines are pointing to the opening at the bottom.  ???  Wait.  That''s not a threshold.  ???  Ok.  Just read the your notes in the Photographer''s Comment section (I usually wait to read those and comments until after I''ve gathered some thoughts).  That''s the top of the door.  Clever.  :-)  Still feels like someone''s in the house... Getting past the trickery and looking at the photo itself.  I like the angles formed by the door and how the bottom right third is separated from the rest.  Personally, I find my attention going to the door handle first before I move anywhere else. In reading the comments I see some questioning the color (i.e. WB off, lose the yellow, etc...).  I''m not quite so bothered by the color, but it does make me wonder (strongly) how this would feel in B&W.  Although B&W may add to the sense of doom and mystery. The other technicals (lighting, focus, DOF) all work fine from where I''m sitting. Certainly an interesting take on the challenge.  Could be good for a revisit if the POV challenge comes back around, eh?  :-) Oops!  One other thing I just noticed as I scrolled up to take a last look at the photo dimensions and file size...did you consider cropping about 1/5 of the image from the top?  I think it would have remained balanced, but when you resize then the image would look larger.  Something to think about. Questions?  Feel free to send me a PM.  Thanks!', '2008-03-04 15:51:18.000000', NULL, 0),
